<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  No-install TableCloth / Windows Sandbox configuration (deep-link template)

  This is the PARAMETERIZED variant of no-install-spork.wsb. It is a TEMPLATE, not a
  ready-to-run file: a producer (browser extension, quick-start web app, MCP server)
  substitutes the two placeholders below and serves or saves the result. Everything else
  (hidden logon shell spawning one visible window, DNS probe then fallback, TLS 1.2,
  Chocolatey style fetch of tablecloth-prepare.ps1, zero MappedFolders, plain text and no
  Base64) is identical to the generic file, so read that one for the rationale.

  Placeholders (see docs/PARAMETERIZED_WSB_SPEC.md section 3.3):

    __SPORK_SITE_IDS__     Optional. Space separated catalog site ids, e.g. 'WooriBank'.
                           Carried to the guest in the TABLECLOTH_SITE_IDS variable.
    __SPORK_TARGET_URL__   Optional. The exact page to open after the plugins are installed,
                           e.g. the page the user was looking at in the host browser.
                           Carried in the TABLECLOTH_TARGET_URL variable.

  Both are optional and independent. Leaving both unsubstituted yields the generic launcher:
  TableCloth treats any value still containing __SPORK as 'not supplied', so a missed
  substitution degrades to the plain catalog UI instead of breaking.

  Escaping rules for the producer (BOTH layers apply, in this order):

    1) XML layer: escape '&', '<' and '>' as '&amp;', '&lt;', '&gt;'. Query strings in the
       real world do contain '&' (for example ...?withyou=CTCER0149&fromSite=pib), so this
       one is not optional. The XML parser restores the original characters before
       PowerShell sees them, and a single quoted PowerShell string treats them literally.
    2) PowerShell / argv layer: the value sits inside nested single quoted strings within a
       double quoted command line, so a literal apostrophe or double quote breaks parsing no
       matter how it is XML escaped. Percent encode them instead: apostrophe as %27 and
       double quote as %22. Other characters need nothing: the outer quotes are argv
       quoting, not a PowerShell string, so '$' and backtick stay literal.

  Also reject, on the producer side, anything that is not an http or https absolute URL,
  anything carrying credentials (user@host form), and anything longer than 2048 characters.

  Trust model: this file can be redistributed by anyone, so the values above are UNTRUSTED
  input by the time the guest sees them. TableCloth re-validates them inside the sandbox:
  the target URL is opened only when it belongs to the same registrable domain as the site
  ids (or, without site ids, when exactly one catalog service matches). Otherwise the URL is
  dropped and the ordinary catalog UI opens. Keeping the URL in plain sight here is
  deliberate: a person inspecting this file must be able to see which page it will open.
-->
<Configuration>
  <Networking>Enable</Networking>
  <vGPU>Disable</vGPU>
  <LogonCommand>
    <Command>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "Start-Process powershell.exe -WindowStyle Normal -ArgumentList '-NoProfile','-ExecutionPolicy','Bypass','-Command','$Host.UI.RawUI.WindowTitle = ''TableCloth Setup''; Write-Host '' Getting TableCloth ready...'' -ForegroundColor Cyan; if (-not (Resolve-DnsName -Name github.com -QuickTimeout -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) { Get-NetAdapter | Where-Object Status -eq ''Up'' | Set-DnsClientServerAddress -ServerAddresses 8.8.8.8,1.1.1.1 }; [Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol = [Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol -bor 3072; $env:TABLECLOTH_SITE_IDS = ''__SPORK_SITE_IDS__''; $env:TABLECLOTH_TARGET_URL = ''__SPORK_TARGET_URL__''; try { iex ((New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString(''https://github.com/yourtablecloth/TableCloth/releases/latest/download/tablecloth-prepare.ps1'')) } catch { Write-Host ('' Failed: '' + $_.Exception.Message) -ForegroundColor Red; $null = Read-Host '' Press Enter to close'' }'"</Command>
  </LogonCommand>
</Configuration>
